    {
      "publicId": "chal_quiz_k8s_151",
      "name": "requests-limits-quiz",
      "type": "quiz",
      "title": "Read the night the api ran out of memory",
      "description": "Three statements about what happened on Rice Rocket's nodes during the monthly report export. Decide whether each one is true.",
      "difficulty": "medium",
      "outcomes": [
        "capacity"
      ],
      "points": 10,
      "hints": [
        "The scheduler never looks at `kubectl top`. Which numbers in the pod spec does it add up against the node's allocatable, and when does it look at them?",
        "Memory and CPU are enforced differently by the kernel. One can be taken back slowly from a process, the other cannot. Which one is which?",
        "Read `lastState.terminated` of the restarted container: the reason and the exit code. 137 is 128 + 9: which signal is 9, and who sends it when a cgroup's memory is full?"
      ],
      "questions": [
        {
          "type": "single_choice",
          "points": 5,
          "question": "Five api pods without any `resources:` ran on one node while the other node was nearly empty:\n\n```text\n$ kubectl get pods -o wide -n rice-rocket\nNAME                   READY   STATUS    NODE\napi-5c9d7f6b8-2xkqz    1/1     Running   pool-a-1\napi-5c9d7f6b8-7hd2m    1/1     Running   pool-a-1\napi-5c9d7f6b8-9pq4r    1/1     Running   pool-a-1\napi-5c9d7f6b8-kd8w1    1/1     Running   pool-a-1\napi-5c9d7f6b8-x7v3n    1/1     Running   pool-a-1\n$ kubectl top nodes\nNAME       CPU(cores)   CPU%   MEMORY(bytes)   MEMORY%\npool-a-1   1810m        93%    3021Mi          89%\npool-a-2   95m          4%     610Mi           18%\n```\n\nStatement: \"The scheduler will move some of these pods to pool-a-2 on its own once it sees pool-a-1 at 93% CPU.\"",
          "explanation": "False. The scheduler places a pod once, when it is created, by adding up the pods' **requests** against each node's allocatable; it never reads live usage and never moves a running pod. Pods with no requests count as zero, so all five fit on pool-a-1. They stay there until they are deleted (or evicted). Setting requests (`cpu: 250m`, `memory: 256Mi`) makes the scheduler spread new pods; a rollout (`kubectl rollout restart deployment/api`) then places them again. The misconception is that Kubernetes balances running load by itself.",
          "options": [
            {
              "text": "True",
              "correct": false
            },
            {
              "text": "False",
              "correct": true
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "single_choice",
          "points": 5,
          "question": "The api container has `limits: { cpu: 500m, memory: 768Mi }`. During a burst of orders:\n\n```text\n$ kubectl top pod api-5c9d7f6b8-2xkqz --containers\nPOD                   NAME   CPU(cores)   MEMORY(bytes)\napi-5c9d7f6b8-2xkqz   api    500m         402Mi\n\n# container_cpu_cfs_throttled_periods_total / container_cpu_cfs_periods_total\napi   0.71\n```\n\nStatement: \"If the burst keeps the api above 500m for long enough, the kubelet restarts the container with OOMKilled, the same way it does for memory.\"",
          "explanation": "False. CPU is a compressible resource: at its limit the kernel's CFS quota **throttles** the container (71% of periods were throttled here) and requests take longer, but the container is never killed or restarted for using CPU. Memory cannot be taken back, so a container over its memory limit is killed (OOMKilled). The fix for this p99 spike is a higher CPU limit or none (keep the request), not more memory. The misconception is that both limits end the same way.",
          "options": [
            {
              "text": "True",
              "correct": false
            },
            {
              "text": "False",
              "correct": true
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "single_choice",
          "points": 5,
          "question": "During the report export the api restarted three times:\n\n```text\n$ kubectl get pod api-5c9d7f6b8-7hd2m -o jsonpath='{.status.containerStatuses[0].lastState}'\n{\"terminated\":{\"exitCode\":137,\"reason\":\"OOMKilled\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-11-03T02:10:41Z\",\"finishedAt\":\"2026-11-03T02:12:07Z\"}}\n$ kubectl get pod api-5c9d7f6b8-7hd2m -o jsonpath='{.spec.containers[0].resources}'\n{\"limits\":{\"memory\":\"768Mi\"},\"requests\":{\"cpu\":\"700m\",\"memory\":\"512Mi\"}}\n```\n\nStatement: \"The container went over its own 768Mi memory limit and the kernel killed it (137 = SIGKILL); the fix is a higher limit or a report export that streams instead of holding every row in memory.\"",
          "explanation": "True. Reason `OOMKilled` with exit code 137 (128 + 9, SIGKILL) means the cgroup of this container hit its memory limit and the kernel's OOM killer ended the process; the kubelet then restarted it. It is not a node problem (that would show as an eviction, `reason: Evicted`). Either the limit is too low for the real peak, or the code holds too much (here: building the whole export in memory). The misconception is to look for an app bug in the logs: an OOM-killed process usually logs nothing at all.",
          "options": [
            {
              "text": "True",
              "correct": true
            },
            {
              "text": "False",
              "correct": false
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "publicId": "chal_coding_k8s_152",
      "name": "node-capacity-planner",
      "type": "mini_coding",
      "title": "Plan a node: requests, QoS, OOMKilled and eviction",
      "description": "Before setting `resources:` on every Rice Rocket workload, the team wants a small tool that shows what one node does with a list of pods. The editor holds `plan-node.js` with its key blocks missing, each marked by a `// [ ]` line; some pieces are wrong on purpose. `planNode(node, pods)` must: read quantities (`250m` and `1` for CPU; `Ki/Mi/Gi` are powers of 1024, `k/M/G` powers of 1000, a bare number is bytes); bind pods in arrival order while the sum of their **requests** fits the node's allocatable (a container with only a limit requests its limit; check CPU first, then memory; a pod that does not fit is pending with reason `Insufficient cpu` or `Insufficient memory`); give every pod a QoS class (`BestEffort` when no container sets any request or limit; `Guaranteed` when every container has CPU and memory limits and its requests equal them; otherwise `Burstable`); for each bound container report `OOMKilled` when its memory usage is above its memory limit, else `Throttled` when its CPU usage is above its CPU limit, else `Running`; and rank the bound pods for eviction under memory pressure: pods using more memory than they request first, then lower `priority` first, then the largest usage minus requests first, then arrival order. Run grades it on scripted nodes and pods.",
      "difficulty": "medium",
      "outcomes": [
        "capacity"
      ],
      "points": 30,
      "hints": [
        "Take the first test on paper: add up what each api pod asks for, not what it uses, and stop when the next pod does not fit into 1930m.",
        "Each helper answers one question: what is this quantity in millicores or bytes, how much does the pod request, which QoS class is it, what happens to a container over a limit, who goes first when memory runs out. The loop only calls them.",
        "A disk planner that binds volumes in order while their requested size fits the free space: `if (want > free) { pending.push({ name, reason: \"Insufficient storage\" }); continue; } free -= want;`, where `want` is `request ?? limit`, and `\"10G\"` is `10 * 1e9` bytes while `\"10Gi\"` is `10 * 1024 ** 3`."
      ],
      "coding": {
        "language": "javascript",
        "filePath": "tools/capacity/plan-node.js",
        "executionMode": "browser",
        "timeLimitMs": 2000,
        "memoryLimitMb": 128,
        "scoringMode": "all_or_nothing",
        "maxAttempts": null
      },
